# Tables in quarto

**Head of data**

If, you have a data frame in R, then can print a table, this way:

```{r, table0}
kable(head(dental), format = "latex", booktabs = TRUE, env = "supertabular", caption = "First observations in wide format") %>%
  kable_styling(latex_options = c("striped", "scale_down", "landscape", "HOLD_position"))
```

Note that in the configuration of chunk is the key: "table0". It is used for make a crossreference of table. For example: "In the table \ref{tab:table0}, we can see the first observatios of the dental dataset" 

The data are presented in a wide format, but to analyze longitudinal data it is importan transform the data into a large format. The following code allows you to do so:

```{r}
dental_long <- pivot_longer(dental, cols = starts_with("y"), 
                            names_to = "measurement", values_to = "distance") %>% 
  mutate(
    age = parse_number(measurement),
    measurement = fct_inorder(paste("Measure at age", age))
  ) %>% 
  set_variable_labels(
    age = "Age of the child at measurement",
    measurement = "Label for time measurement",
    distance = "Measurement"
  )
```

```{r, table1}
kable(head(dental_long, 10), format = "latex", booktabs = TRUE, env = "supertabular", caption = "First observations in large format") %>%
  kable_styling(latex_options = c("striped", "scale_down", "landscape", "HOLD_position"))
```

In the \ref{tab:table1}, note that the data had changed to large format. 

The argument "HOLD_position" in latex_options sets the position of tables and "hold_position" allows a table to float. You can see more information in the arguments of "kable_styling" function. 


