# Plots in quarto

To create plots in quarto is very easy, the structure is the same that rmarkdown. The next code show how to create a plot:

```{r}
#| label: fig-figura0
#| fig-cap: "Boxplots"
ggplot(dental_long, aes(measurement, distance, fill = measurement)) +
  geom_boxplot() +
  geom_jitter(width = 0.2) +
  guides(fill = "none") +
  labs(x = "", y = "Dental growth, mm")
```

If you have many plots and wish to put in the same shape, you can use this code:

```{r, fig.pos="H"}
#| label: fig-figura2
#| fig-cap: "Two plots"
#| fig-subcap:
#|  - "First plot"
#|  - "Second plot"
#| layout-ncol: 2
ggplot(dental_long, aes(measurement, distance, fill = measurement)) +
  geom_boxplot() +
  geom_jitter(width = 0.2) +
  guides(fill = "none") +
  labs(x = "", y = "Dental growth, mm")
ggplot(dental_long, aes(sex, distance, fill = measurement)) +
  geom_boxplot() +
  labs(x = "", y = "Dental growth, mm", fill = "")
```


The argument "fig.pos="H"" in the chunk sets the position of figures and "fig.pos="h"" allows a figure to float. 


# Equations in quarto

In statistic the equations are very important for describe relations, situations, models, theories, cumputes, events, sujets, among others things. In Quarto the way for to expressing equations is:

$$
\mathbf{y} = \mathbf{X}\boldsymbol{\beta} + \mathbf{Z}\mathbf{u} + \boldsymbol{\epsilon}
$$ {#eq-ecuacion1}

where:

```{=latex}
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\mathbf{y}$ is the vector of observed responses.
    \item $\mathbf{X}$ is the design matrix for the fixed effects.
    \item $\boldsymbol{\beta}$ is the vector of fixed-effects coefficients.
    \item $\mathbf{Z}$ is the design matrix for the random effects.
    \item $\mathbf{u}$ is the vector of random-effects coefficients.
    \item $\boldsymbol{\epsilon}$ is the vector of residual errors.
\end{itemize}
```

The key is "equation1". To create a cross-reference, we use the following: The equation @eq-ecuacion1 show a linear mixed model. 


